1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,857
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,037 --> 00:00:03,855
Most of us learned about money the

3
00:00:03,855 --> 00:00:04,336
hard way.

4
00:00:04,516 --> 00:00:05,864
A first credit card,

5
00:00:05,864 --> 00:00:07,010
a first car loan,

6
00:00:07,010 --> 00:00:09,437
a first month where the paycheck ran

7
00:00:09,437 --> 00:00:11,122
out before the bills did.

8
00:00:11,302 --> 00:00:13,715
Nobody sat us down and explained how

9
00:00:13,715 --> 00:00:14,855
any of it worked.

10
00:00:15,035 --> 00:00:16,742
But in many wealthy families,

11
00:00:16,742 --> 00:00:19,038
kids learn these lessons at the kitchen

12
00:00:19,038 --> 00:00:21,157
table, years before they ever earn a

13
00:00:21,157 --> 00:00:21,687
paycheck.

14
00:00:21,867 --> 00:00:24,715
Today, twenty five money lessons rich parents

15
00:00:24,715 --> 00:00:25,791
teach their kids.

16
00:00:25,971 --> 00:00:27,318
And here's the good news.

17
00:00:27,498 --> 00:00:29,405
You can teach them to your kids,

18
00:00:29,405 --> 00:00:31,312
and you can learn them yourself,

19
00:00:31,312 --> 00:00:31,968
at any age.

20
00:00:32,318 --> 00:00:34,314
Here's why this matters so much.

21
00:00:34,494 --> 00:00:37,340
Researchers at the University of Cambridge,

22
00:00:37,340 --> 00:00:39,259
in a study for the UK's Money

23
00:00:39,259 --> 00:00:42,039
Advice Service, found that many core money

24
00:00:42,039 --> 00:00:44,686
habits are already forming by around age

25
00:00:44,686 --> 00:00:45,083
seven.

26
00:00:45,433 --> 00:00:46,292
Think about that.

27
00:00:46,472 --> 00:00:48,198
By the time a child is in

28
00:00:48,198 --> 00:00:51,649
second grade, they're already picking up attitudes

29
00:00:51,649 --> 00:00:54,756
about saving, spending and waiting for things

30
00:00:54,756 --> 00:00:55,446
they want.

31
00:00:55,626 --> 00:00:57,881
Most of those attitudes come from watching

32
00:00:57,881 --> 00:00:58,633
their parents.

33
00:00:58,813 --> 00:00:59,986
Not from lectures.

34
00:01:00,166 --> 00:01:02,440
From what happens in the grocery store,

35
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:04,539
at the car dealership and around the

36
00:01:04,539 --> 00:01:05,297
dinner table.

37
00:01:05,477 --> 00:01:07,411
That's why the money lessons in a

38
00:01:07,411 --> 00:01:08,759
family get passed down,

39
00:01:08,759 --> 00:01:10,400
generation after generation,

40
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:12,685
whether anyone intends to teach them or

41
00:01:12,685 --> 00:01:12,919
not.

42
00:01:13,270 --> 00:01:15,669
We'll look at these twenty five lessons

43
00:01:15,669 --> 00:01:16,776
from three angles.

44
00:01:16,956 --> 00:01:18,442
First, the numbers.

45
00:01:18,622 --> 00:01:20,754
The basic math of money that many

46
00:01:20,754 --> 00:01:22,628
wealthy families teach early,

47
00:01:22,628 --> 00:01:25,472
like compound interest and opportunity cost.

48
00:01:25,652 --> 00:01:27,602
Second, the psychology.

49
00:01:27,782 --> 00:01:29,372
The attitudes about waiting,

50
00:01:29,372 --> 00:01:31,758
comparing and enough that shape a lifetime

51
00:01:31,758 --> 00:01:32,496
of decisions.

52
00:01:32,676 --> 00:01:34,359
And third, the playbook.

53
00:01:34,539 --> 00:01:35,896
The practical tools,

54
00:01:35,896 --> 00:01:39,153
from first investment accounts to credit scores,

55
00:01:39,153 --> 00:01:41,052
that give kids a head start.

56
00:01:41,232 --> 00:01:42,529
Whether you're a parent,

57
00:01:42,529 --> 00:01:44,852
a grandparent, or someone who wishes they'd

58
00:01:44,852 --> 00:01:46,094
learned this at twelve,

59
00:01:46,094 --> 00:01:47,769
there's something here for you.

60
00:01:48,119 --> 00:01:49,709
Let's start with the numbers.

61
00:01:49,889 --> 00:01:52,908
Lesson one is the foundation of everything

62
00:01:52,908 --> 00:01:53,268
else.

63
00:01:53,618 --> 00:01:54,616
Our sources.

64
00:01:54,796 --> 00:01:57,111
The research on when money habits form

65
00:01:57,111 --> 00:01:59,914
comes from the University of Cambridge's study

66
00:01:59,914 --> 00:02:01,681
for the Money Advice Service.

67
00:02:01,861 --> 00:02:04,249
Data on how families talk about money

68
00:02:04,249 --> 00:02:05,088
comes from T.

69
00:02:05,268 --> 00:02:06,703
Rowe Price's Parents,

70
00:02:06,703 --> 00:02:08,206
Kids and Money Survey.

71
00:02:08,386 --> 00:02:10,923
Research on giving money to adult children

72
00:02:10,923 --> 00:02:13,279
comes from Thomas Stanley's work in The

73
00:02:13,279 --> 00:02:14,609
Millionaire Next Door.

74
00:02:14,789 --> 00:02:17,099
Account rules come from the IRS.

75
00:02:17,279 --> 00:02:20,266
Growth examples use a stated seven percent

76
00:02:20,266 --> 00:02:21,831
average yearly return,

77
00:02:21,831 --> 00:02:24,036
an illustration, not a promise.

78
00:02:24,216 --> 00:02:25,539
This is education,

79
00:02:25,539 --> 00:02:27,084
not financial advice.

80
00:02:27,434 --> 00:02:28,142
Angle one.

81
00:02:28,322 --> 00:02:28,949
The numbers.

82
00:02:29,129 --> 00:02:32,079
Nine lessons about how money actually works,

83
00:02:32,079 --> 00:02:34,492
taught in ways a kid can understand.

84
00:02:34,842 --> 00:02:36,096
Lesson number one.

85
00:02:36,276 --> 00:02:38,676
Money comes from creating value for other

86
00:02:38,676 --> 00:02:39,086
people.

87
00:02:39,436 --> 00:02:41,803
Many wealthy parents make sure their kids

88
00:02:41,803 --> 00:02:43,766
understand where money comes from.

89
00:02:43,946 --> 00:02:46,122
It doesn't come from the ATM or

90
00:02:46,122 --> 00:02:46,965
from a card.

91
00:02:47,145 --> 00:02:49,934
It comes from someone doing something valuable

92
00:02:49,934 --> 00:02:50,964
for someone else.

93
00:02:51,144 --> 00:02:52,489
That might be a job,

94
00:02:52,489 --> 00:02:54,707
a business, or an investment in a

95
00:02:54,707 --> 00:02:55,312
business.

96
00:02:55,492 --> 00:02:57,739
Kids who understand this early tend to

97
00:02:57,739 --> 00:03:00,044
see money as something they can create,

98
00:03:00,044 --> 00:03:01,936
not just something they receive.

99
00:03:02,286 --> 00:03:05,021
Some families pay for extra chores beyond

100
00:03:05,021 --> 00:03:07,357
the basics, like washing the car or

101
00:03:07,357 --> 00:03:08,892
helping with yard work.

102
00:03:09,072 --> 00:03:11,725
Others encourage teenagers to get part time

103
00:03:11,725 --> 00:03:14,132
jobs, even when the family doesn't need

104
00:03:14,132 --> 00:03:14,749
the money.

105
00:03:14,929 --> 00:03:16,485
The point isn't the amount.

106
00:03:16,665 --> 00:03:18,931
It's the connection between effort,

107
00:03:18,931 --> 00:03:20,032
value and reward.

108
00:03:20,212 --> 00:03:22,380
A teenager who earns their first hundred

109
00:03:22,380 --> 00:03:24,819
dollars often treats it very differently from

110
00:03:24,819 --> 00:03:26,988
a hundred dollars that was simply handed

111
00:03:26,988 --> 00:03:27,259
over.

112
00:03:27,609 --> 00:03:28,665
Teach it this week.

113
00:03:28,845 --> 00:03:31,264
Pick one extra job around the house

114
00:03:31,264 --> 00:03:33,199
that a child can do for pay.

115
00:03:33,379 --> 00:03:34,981
Talk about why it's worth money.

116
00:03:35,161 --> 00:03:37,526
Someone would otherwise have to do it,

117
00:03:37,526 --> 00:03:38,957
or pay someone else to.

118
00:03:39,137 --> 00:03:41,460
If you're an adult learning this lesson

119
00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:43,961
yourself, ask the same question about your

120
00:03:43,961 --> 00:03:44,199
job.

121
00:03:44,379 --> 00:03:45,955
What value do I create,

122
00:03:45,955 --> 00:03:47,874
and how could I create more?

123
00:03:48,224 --> 00:03:49,512
Lesson number two.

124
00:03:49,692 --> 00:03:52,281
Save first, then spend what's left.

125
00:03:52,632 --> 00:03:55,641
A classic teaching tool is three jars,

126
00:03:55,641 --> 00:03:57,937
labeled save, spend and give.

127
00:03:58,117 --> 00:04:00,181
Every time a child gets money,

128
00:04:00,181 --> 00:04:01,970
whether it's an allowance,

129
00:04:01,970 --> 00:04:03,758
a gift or pay for a chore,

130
00:04:03,758 --> 00:04:06,716
it's split between the jars before anything

131
00:04:06,716 --> 00:04:07,336
is spent.

132
00:04:07,516 --> 00:04:10,475
It's simple, visual and surprisingly powerful,

133
00:04:10,475 --> 00:04:12,855
because it builds the habit of paying

134
00:04:12,855 --> 00:04:15,364
yourself first before a child ever sees

135
00:04:15,364 --> 00:04:16,072
a paycheck.

136
00:04:16,423 --> 00:04:18,520
Adults who pay themselves first,

137
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:21,339
by automatically moving money to savings on

138
00:04:21,339 --> 00:04:23,568
payday, tend to save far more than

139
00:04:23,568 --> 00:04:25,797
those who save whatever is left at

140
00:04:25,797 --> 00:04:27,173
the end of the month.

141
00:04:27,353 --> 00:04:29,786
The three jars are the childhood version

142
00:04:29,786 --> 00:04:31,185
of exactly that system.

143
00:04:31,365 --> 00:04:33,261
Kids who grow up with it often

144
00:04:33,261 --> 00:04:36,485
find automatic saving completely natural as adults.

145
00:04:36,835 --> 00:04:37,810
Teach it this week.

146
00:04:37,990 --> 00:04:40,730
Set up three jars or three envelopes.

147
00:04:40,910 --> 00:04:42,860
Decide on a split together,

148
00:04:42,860 --> 00:04:45,171
for example fifty percent spend,

149
00:04:45,171 --> 00:04:46,543
forty percent save,

150
00:04:46,543 --> 00:04:47,771
ten percent give.

151
00:04:47,951 --> 00:04:49,795
Let the child choose what to do

152
00:04:49,795 --> 00:04:50,925
with the spend jar,

153
00:04:50,925 --> 00:04:53,245
even if they make choices you wouldn't.

154
00:04:53,425 --> 00:04:56,361
For yourself, set up one automatic transfer

155
00:04:56,361 --> 00:04:58,545
to savings the day after payday.

156
00:04:58,896 --> 00:05:00,161
Lesson number three.

157
00:05:00,341 --> 00:05:01,929
Money can grow on its own,

158
00:05:01,929 --> 00:05:03,151
if you give it time.

159
00:05:03,501 --> 00:05:06,031
Compound interest is when your money earns

160
00:05:06,031 --> 00:05:08,681
returns, and then those returns earn returns

161
00:05:08,681 --> 00:05:08,922
too.

162
00:05:09,102 --> 00:05:11,343
Over time, the growth snowballs.

163
00:05:11,523 --> 00:05:13,929
Many wealthy parents explain it early,

164
00:05:13,929 --> 00:05:16,779
because time is the most powerful ingredient,

165
00:05:16,779 --> 00:05:19,058
and kids have more time than anyone.

166
00:05:19,238 --> 00:05:21,789
A dollar invested at fifteen has fifty

167
00:05:21,789 --> 00:05:24,542
years to grow before a typical retirement

168
00:05:24,542 --> 00:05:24,811
age.

169
00:05:25,161 --> 00:05:27,337
Some parents make it concrete with a

170
00:05:27,337 --> 00:05:28,063
family bank.

171
00:05:28,243 --> 00:05:30,429
They offer a generous interest rate on

172
00:05:30,429 --> 00:05:32,328
money the child leaves with them,

173
00:05:32,328 --> 00:05:33,421
maybe paid monthly.

174
00:05:33,601 --> 00:05:36,756
Watching a balance grow without doing anything

175
00:05:36,756 --> 00:05:38,814
is a magical moment for a kid.

176
00:05:38,994 --> 00:05:41,465
It turns an abstract idea into something

177
00:05:41,465 --> 00:05:42,268
they can see.

178
00:05:42,448 --> 00:05:44,865
Others show a simple chart of what

179
00:05:44,865 --> 00:05:48,276
a small monthly investment becomes over decades.

180
00:05:48,627 --> 00:05:49,683
Teach it this week.

181
00:05:49,863 --> 00:05:52,818
Use a free online compound interest calculator

182
00:05:52,818 --> 00:05:53,845
with your child.

183
00:05:54,025 --> 00:05:56,371
Try one hundred dollars a month at

184
00:05:56,371 --> 00:05:58,993
a seven percent average yearly return,

185
00:05:58,993 --> 00:06:01,200
for ten, twenty and fifty years.

186
00:06:01,380 --> 00:06:03,104
Let them see how the line curves

187
00:06:03,104 --> 00:06:03,482
upward.

188
00:06:03,662 --> 00:06:06,032
For yourself, run the same numbers on

189
00:06:06,032 --> 00:06:07,121
your own savings.

190
00:06:07,472 --> 00:06:08,981
Here's what that looks like.

191
00:06:09,161 --> 00:06:11,054
One hundred dollars a month,

192
00:06:11,054 --> 00:06:13,894
starting at age fifteen and invested until

193
00:06:13,894 --> 00:06:16,464
sixty five, at a seven percent average

194
00:06:16,464 --> 00:06:19,034
yearly return, could grow to over five

195
00:06:19,034 --> 00:06:20,724
hundred thousand dollars.

196
00:06:20,904 --> 00:06:21,966
Total put in,

197
00:06:21,966 --> 00:06:23,192
sixty thousand.

198
00:06:23,542 --> 00:06:24,865
Lesson number four.

199
00:06:25,045 --> 00:06:27,170
Interest works against you when you borrow.

200
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:29,764
The same math that grows savings can

201
00:06:29,764 --> 00:06:30,387
grow debt.

202
00:06:30,567 --> 00:06:32,739
Wealthy parents often make sure their kids

203
00:06:32,739 --> 00:06:34,962
understand this before they get their first

204
00:06:34,962 --> 00:06:35,583
credit card.

205
00:06:35,763 --> 00:06:38,077
A card balance at over twenty percent

206
00:06:38,077 --> 00:06:40,704
interest, which has been typical in recent

207
00:06:40,704 --> 00:06:43,143
years according to the Federal Reserve,

208
00:06:43,143 --> 00:06:45,207
can grow quickly if you only make

209
00:06:45,207 --> 00:06:46,270
minimum payments.

210
00:06:46,620 --> 00:06:48,578
A powerful way to teach this is

211
00:06:48,578 --> 00:06:50,410
to let a child borrow a small

212
00:06:50,410 --> 00:06:51,421
amount from you,

213
00:06:51,421 --> 00:06:53,190
for something they want now,

214
00:06:53,190 --> 00:06:54,770
and charge them interest.

215
00:06:54,950 --> 00:06:56,965
When they have to pay back twelve

216
00:06:56,965 --> 00:06:58,797
dollars for a ten dollar loan,

217
00:06:58,797 --> 00:07:01,056
the lesson sticks far better than any

218
00:07:01,056 --> 00:07:01,544
lecture.

219
00:07:01,724 --> 00:07:03,869
Some families do the reverse too,

220
00:07:03,869 --> 00:07:05,754
and show how long it takes to

221
00:07:05,754 --> 00:07:08,354
pay off a balance with minimum payments.

222
00:07:08,704 --> 00:07:09,621
Teach it this week.

223
00:07:09,801 --> 00:07:11,869
Look at a real credit card statement

224
00:07:11,869 --> 00:07:13,938
together and find the box that shows

225
00:07:13,938 --> 00:07:15,605
how long it would take to pay

226
00:07:15,605 --> 00:07:17,788
off the balance with minimum payments.

227
00:07:17,968 --> 00:07:20,253
For yourself, list every debt you have

228
00:07:20,253 --> 00:07:21,636
with its interest rate,

229
00:07:21,636 --> 00:07:23,680
and target the highest rate first.

230
00:07:24,031 --> 00:07:25,342
Lesson number five.

231
00:07:25,522 --> 00:07:27,614
The price isn't the same as the

232
00:07:27,614 --> 00:07:28,019
value.

233
00:07:28,369 --> 00:07:30,892
Rich parents often teach kids to think

234
00:07:30,892 --> 00:07:32,153
about cost per use.

235
00:07:32,333 --> 00:07:34,554
A sixty dollar pair of shoes worn

236
00:07:34,554 --> 00:07:37,245
three hundred times costs twenty cents a

237
00:07:37,245 --> 00:07:37,581
wear.

238
00:07:37,761 --> 00:07:40,351
A thirty dollar pair worn five times

239
00:07:40,351 --> 00:07:42,150
costs six dollars a wear.

240
00:07:42,330 --> 00:07:45,290
The cheaper item was actually more expensive.

241
00:07:45,470 --> 00:07:48,169
This simple idea helps kids judge purchases

242
00:07:48,169 --> 00:07:50,428
by how much they'll really use them.

243
00:07:50,778 --> 00:07:52,670
It also works the other way.

244
00:07:52,850 --> 00:07:55,215
Some things are expensive and still not

245
00:07:55,215 --> 00:07:57,459
worth it, because they won't be used.

246
00:07:57,639 --> 00:07:59,404
The trendy gadget that sits in a

247
00:07:59,404 --> 00:08:00,507
drawer after a week.

248
00:08:00,687 --> 00:08:02,647
The toy that's forgotten by the next

249
00:08:02,647 --> 00:08:03,136
birthday.

250
00:08:03,316 --> 00:08:05,200
Asking how often will I use this

251
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:07,083
before buying is one of the most

252
00:08:07,083 --> 00:08:09,319
useful money habits a person can have.

253
00:08:09,669 --> 00:08:10,574
Teach it this week.

254
00:08:10,754 --> 00:08:12,795
Before your child's next purchase,

255
00:08:12,795 --> 00:08:14,715
ask them to guess how many times

256
00:08:14,715 --> 00:08:15,616
they'll use it,

257
00:08:15,616 --> 00:08:17,836
then divide the price by that number.

258
00:08:18,016 --> 00:08:20,174
For yourself, try it on the five

259
00:08:20,174 --> 00:08:22,804
most expensive things in your closet or

260
00:08:22,804 --> 00:08:23,276
garage.

261
00:08:23,626 --> 00:08:25,065
Lesson number six.

262
00:08:25,245 --> 00:08:26,317
Every time you spend,

263
00:08:26,317 --> 00:08:27,950
you're giving something else up.

264
00:08:28,301 --> 00:08:31,075
Economists call it opportunity cost.

265
00:08:31,255 --> 00:08:33,346
If you spend twenty dollars on candy,

266
00:08:33,346 --> 00:08:35,493
you can't spend that twenty dollars on

267
00:08:35,493 --> 00:08:37,188
a game you've been saving for.

268
00:08:37,368 --> 00:08:39,967
Wealthy parents often teach this by making

269
00:08:39,967 --> 00:08:42,256
kids choose, rather than buying both.

270
00:08:42,436 --> 00:08:44,403
The question becomes not can I afford

271
00:08:44,403 --> 00:08:45,944
this, but what am I giving up

272
00:08:45,944 --> 00:08:46,476
to get it?

273
00:08:46,826 --> 00:08:49,964
For adults, the biggest opportunity cost is

274
00:08:49,964 --> 00:08:51,423
often future growth.

275
00:08:51,603 --> 00:08:53,828
A thousand dollars spent today is not

276
00:08:53,828 --> 00:08:55,272
just a thousand dollars.

277
00:08:55,452 --> 00:08:57,796
At a seven percent average yearly return,

278
00:08:57,796 --> 00:09:00,139
it could have roughly doubled about every

279
00:09:00,139 --> 00:09:00,711
ten years.

280
00:09:00,891 --> 00:09:03,403
That's why wealthy people often think twice

281
00:09:03,403 --> 00:09:04,630
before big purchases.

282
00:09:04,980 --> 00:09:05,943
Teach it this week.

283
00:09:06,123 --> 00:09:06,921
At the store,

284
00:09:06,921 --> 00:09:09,007
give your child a fixed amount and

285
00:09:09,007 --> 00:09:11,401
let them choose between two things they

286
00:09:11,401 --> 00:09:11,708
want.

287
00:09:11,888 --> 00:09:13,234
Talk about what they gave up.

288
00:09:13,414 --> 00:09:15,802
For yourself, before any purchase over a

289
00:09:15,802 --> 00:09:18,130
hundred dollars, say out loud what else

290
00:09:18,130 --> 00:09:19,324
that money could do.

291
00:09:19,674 --> 00:09:21,009
Lesson number seven.

292
00:09:21,189 --> 00:09:23,103
A budget is just a plan for

293
00:09:23,103 --> 00:09:23,882
your money.

294
00:09:24,233 --> 00:09:27,476
Many financially savvy parents use an allowance

295
00:09:27,476 --> 00:09:28,787
as a teaching tool,

296
00:09:28,787 --> 00:09:30,305
not just pocket money.

297
00:09:30,485 --> 00:09:31,691
As kids get older,

298
00:09:31,691 --> 00:09:33,032
the allowance grows,

299
00:09:33,032 --> 00:09:35,245
and so do their responsibilities.

300
00:09:35,425 --> 00:09:37,629
A teenager might get a monthly amount

301
00:09:37,629 --> 00:09:39,178
that covers their clothes,

302
00:09:39,178 --> 00:09:40,607
entertainment and phone,

303
00:09:40,607 --> 00:09:42,216
and has to plan how to make

304
00:09:42,216 --> 00:09:42,693
it last.

305
00:09:43,043 --> 00:09:45,683
This lets kids practice budgeting with small

306
00:09:45,683 --> 00:09:47,663
amounts, when mistakes are cheap.

307
00:09:47,843 --> 00:09:49,898
Running out of money in the middle

308
00:09:49,898 --> 00:09:51,590
of the month at fifteen is a

309
00:09:51,590 --> 00:09:52,557
valuable lesson.

310
00:09:52,737 --> 00:09:54,557
Running out at thirty five,

311
00:09:54,557 --> 00:09:55,838
with rent and kids,

312
00:09:55,838 --> 00:09:57,253
is much more painful.

313
00:09:57,604 --> 00:09:58,672
Teach it this week.

314
00:09:58,852 --> 00:10:00,797
If your child is old enough,

315
00:10:00,797 --> 00:10:03,715
give them responsibility for one category,

316
00:10:03,715 --> 00:10:05,800
like clothes or entertainment,

317
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:07,745
with a fixed monthly amount.

318
00:10:07,925 --> 00:10:10,166
Let them plan and make mistakes.

319
00:10:10,346 --> 00:10:12,651
For yourself, write a one page plan

320
00:10:12,651 --> 00:10:14,165
for next month's money.

321
00:10:14,516 --> 00:10:15,665
Lesson number eight.

322
00:10:15,845 --> 00:10:17,408
What you earn isn't what you take

323
00:10:17,408 --> 00:10:17,645
home.

324
00:10:17,995 --> 00:10:20,001
Many young adults are shocked by their

325
00:10:20,001 --> 00:10:20,793
first paycheck.

326
00:10:20,973 --> 00:10:22,610
The salary was one number,

327
00:10:22,610 --> 00:10:24,688
but the deposit was much smaller.

328
00:10:24,868 --> 00:10:26,331
Federal income tax,

329
00:10:26,331 --> 00:10:28,333
Social Security, Medicare,

330
00:10:28,333 --> 00:10:29,565
maybe state tax,

331
00:10:29,565 --> 00:10:33,722
health insurance and retirement contributions all come

332
00:10:33,722 --> 00:10:34,492
out first.

333
00:10:34,672 --> 00:10:37,438
Wealthy parents often walk their kids through

334
00:10:37,438 --> 00:10:39,465
a real pay stub before that first

335
00:10:39,465 --> 00:10:39,711
job.

336
00:10:40,062 --> 00:10:42,957
Understanding gross versus net pay changes how

337
00:10:42,957 --> 00:10:44,216
young people budget,

338
00:10:44,216 --> 00:10:46,482
and how they think about job offers.

339
00:10:46,662 --> 00:10:48,324
It also introduces taxes,

340
00:10:48,324 --> 00:10:50,452
which play a huge role in wealth

341
00:10:50,452 --> 00:10:51,050
building.

342
00:10:51,230 --> 00:10:53,730
Kids who learn early that tax advantaged

343
00:10:53,730 --> 00:10:56,354
accounts exist often start using them much

344
00:10:56,354 --> 00:10:56,792
sooner.

345
00:10:57,142 --> 00:10:58,175
Teach it this week.

346
00:10:58,355 --> 00:11:00,676
Show your teenager one of your pay

347
00:11:00,676 --> 00:11:02,725
stubs, or a sample one online,

348
00:11:02,725 --> 00:11:04,090
and go line by line.

349
00:11:04,270 --> 00:11:05,663
What is each deduction?

350
00:11:05,843 --> 00:11:06,877
Where does it go?

351
00:11:07,057 --> 00:11:09,454
For yourself, check whether you're getting the

352
00:11:09,454 --> 00:11:11,747
full employer match on your retirement plan.

353
00:11:12,097 --> 00:11:13,292
Lesson number nine.

354
00:11:13,472 --> 00:11:14,912
Owners build wealth.

355
00:11:15,092 --> 00:11:16,416
Customers pay for it.

356
00:11:16,766 --> 00:11:18,544
When a kid buys a video game,

357
00:11:18,544 --> 00:11:20,690
they're a customer of that company.

358
00:11:20,870 --> 00:11:22,970
When they own shares of that company,

359
00:11:22,970 --> 00:11:24,446
they share in its profits.

360
00:11:24,626 --> 00:11:27,598
Wealthy families often teach this distinction early.

361
00:11:27,778 --> 00:11:30,005
They point out that broad index funds

362
00:11:30,005 --> 00:11:32,352
let ordinary people own small pieces of

363
00:11:32,352 --> 00:11:33,676
hundreds of companies.

364
00:11:34,026 --> 00:11:36,162
Some parents buy a single share of

365
00:11:36,162 --> 00:11:37,922
a company their child loves,

366
00:11:37,922 --> 00:11:39,807
so the child can follow it and

367
00:11:39,807 --> 00:11:41,567
feel what it's like to be an

368
00:11:41,567 --> 00:11:41,944
owner.

369
00:11:42,124 --> 00:11:43,865
The goal isn't stock picking.

370
00:11:44,045 --> 00:11:46,224
It's understanding that you can be on

371
00:11:46,224 --> 00:11:48,109
the earning side of the economy,

372
00:11:48,109 --> 00:11:49,699
not just the spending side.

373
00:11:50,049 --> 00:11:51,186
Teach it this week.

374
00:11:51,366 --> 00:11:53,628
Pick a few companies whose products your

375
00:11:53,628 --> 00:11:54,873
family uses every day.

376
00:11:55,053 --> 00:11:57,069
Look up their stock prices together and

377
00:11:57,069 --> 00:11:58,672
talk about what it means to own

378
00:11:58,672 --> 00:11:59,499
a piece of them.

379
00:11:59,679 --> 00:12:02,045
For yourself, ask what you own that

380
00:12:02,045 --> 00:12:03,870
generates income or growth.

381
00:12:04,221 --> 00:12:05,417
Before we move on,

382
00:12:05,417 --> 00:12:08,142
let's talk about why these number lessons

383
00:12:08,142 --> 00:12:09,538
so often get skipped.

384
00:12:09,718 --> 00:12:11,181
In surveys by T.

385
00:12:11,361 --> 00:12:13,644
Rowe Price, many parents have said they

386
00:12:13,644 --> 00:12:16,513
feel uncomfortable talking about money with their

387
00:12:16,513 --> 00:12:16,806
kids.

388
00:12:16,986 --> 00:12:18,843
Some feel they don't know enough.

389
00:12:19,023 --> 00:12:21,485
Others were raised to think money talk

390
00:12:21,485 --> 00:12:22,263
is impolite.

391
00:12:22,443 --> 00:12:24,219
Some don't want to worry their children.

392
00:12:24,569 --> 00:12:26,449
But silence teaches too.

393
00:12:26,629 --> 00:12:28,859
When parents never talk about money,

394
00:12:28,859 --> 00:12:31,088
kids still learn from what they see.

395
00:12:31,268 --> 00:12:32,535
They see the new car,

396
00:12:32,535 --> 00:12:33,622
the shopping bags,

397
00:12:33,622 --> 00:12:35,494
the stress when a bill arrives.

398
00:12:35,674 --> 00:12:38,542
Without explanations, they fill in the blanks

399
00:12:38,542 --> 00:12:41,664
themselves, and often draw the wrong conclusions.

400
00:12:42,015 --> 00:12:43,660
You don't need to be an expert

401
00:12:43,660 --> 00:12:45,196
to have these conversations.

402
00:12:45,376 --> 00:12:46,839
You just need to be honest.

403
00:12:47,019 --> 00:12:49,205
Saying I made a mistake with credit

404
00:12:49,205 --> 00:12:50,642
cards when I was young,

405
00:12:50,642 --> 00:12:52,266
and here's what I learned,

406
00:12:52,266 --> 00:12:54,202
can be one of the most valuable

407
00:12:54,202 --> 00:12:55,889
lessons a child ever hears.

408
00:12:56,069 --> 00:12:58,456
Your mistakes are free tuition for your

409
00:12:58,456 --> 00:12:58,762
kids.

410
00:12:59,112 --> 00:12:59,913
That's angle one.

411
00:13:00,093 --> 00:13:01,342
Money is earned,

412
00:13:01,342 --> 00:13:03,684
save first, compound interest,

413
00:13:03,684 --> 00:13:05,011
interest on debt,

414
00:13:05,011 --> 00:13:06,494
price versus value,

415
00:13:06,494 --> 00:13:08,680
opportunity cost, budgeting,

416
00:13:08,680 --> 00:13:10,553
paychecks and ownership.

417
00:13:10,733 --> 00:13:11,917
Now angle two.

418
00:13:12,097 --> 00:13:13,200
The psychology.

419
00:13:13,380 --> 00:13:15,505
Because knowing the math isn't enough.

420
00:13:15,685 --> 00:13:18,079
The attitudes are what really get passed

421
00:13:18,079 --> 00:13:18,379
down.

422
00:13:18,729 --> 00:13:21,190
The most important money lessons often aren't

423
00:13:21,190 --> 00:13:22,339
about numbers at all.

424
00:13:22,519 --> 00:13:23,956
They're about patience,

425
00:13:23,956 --> 00:13:26,518
comparison, security and what it means to

426
00:13:26,518 --> 00:13:27,268
have enough.

427
00:13:27,448 --> 00:13:30,230
Rich parents who raise financially healthy kids

428
00:13:30,230 --> 00:13:32,005
tend to focus as much on these

429
00:13:32,005 --> 00:13:33,485
attitudes as on the math.

430
00:13:33,835 --> 00:13:35,007
Lesson number ten.

431
00:13:35,187 --> 00:13:36,162
Learning to wait.

432
00:13:36,513 --> 00:13:37,852
When kids want something,

433
00:13:37,852 --> 00:13:39,996
wealthy parents often don't just buy it.

434
00:13:40,176 --> 00:13:41,720
They help the child save for it.

435
00:13:41,900 --> 00:13:43,963
Waiting weeks or months for a toy

436
00:13:43,963 --> 00:13:47,090
teaches patience, planning and the satisfaction of

437
00:13:47,090 --> 00:13:48,216
earning something.

438
00:13:48,396 --> 00:13:50,777
It's the childhood version of saving for

439
00:13:50,777 --> 00:13:52,087
a house or retirement.

440
00:13:52,438 --> 00:13:55,606
The famous marshmallow studies suggested that children

441
00:13:55,606 --> 00:13:57,600
who could wait for a bigger reward

442
00:13:57,600 --> 00:13:59,067
did better later in life.

443
00:13:59,247 --> 00:14:01,441
Newer research has added nuance,

444
00:14:01,441 --> 00:14:04,320
showing family circumstances matter a lot.

445
00:14:04,500 --> 00:14:06,421
But the skill of waiting is still

446
00:14:06,421 --> 00:14:08,808
something families can practice together.

447
00:14:09,158 --> 00:14:10,226
Teach it this week.

448
00:14:10,406 --> 00:14:12,732
Next time your child asks for something,

449
00:14:12,732 --> 00:14:14,710
make a savings chart and help them

450
00:14:14,710 --> 00:14:16,513
track their progress toward it.

451
00:14:16,693 --> 00:14:18,952
For yourself, try a thirty day wait

452
00:14:18,952 --> 00:14:21,534
before any non essential purchase over a

453
00:14:21,534 --> 00:14:22,567
hundred dollars.

454
00:14:22,918 --> 00:14:24,288
Lesson number eleven.

455
00:14:24,468 --> 00:14:26,209
Someone will always have more.

456
00:14:26,559 --> 00:14:28,346
Kids notice what their friends have.

457
00:14:28,526 --> 00:14:29,593
The newer phone,

458
00:14:29,593 --> 00:14:30,726
the bigger house,

459
00:14:30,726 --> 00:14:31,859
the cooler shoes.

460
00:14:32,039 --> 00:14:34,794
Wealthy parents often teach that comparison is

461
00:14:34,794 --> 00:14:37,370
a trap, because there's always someone with

462
00:14:37,370 --> 00:14:37,669
more.

463
00:14:37,849 --> 00:14:39,683
They focus their kids on their own

464
00:14:39,683 --> 00:14:40,438
goals instead.

465
00:14:40,789 --> 00:14:43,563
Many quiet millionaires model this themselves.

466
00:14:43,743 --> 00:14:45,879
They live in modest homes and drive

467
00:14:45,879 --> 00:14:48,442
ordinary cars, even when they could afford

468
00:14:48,442 --> 00:14:48,747
more.

469
00:14:48,927 --> 00:14:50,481
Their kids learn that you don't need

470
00:14:50,481 --> 00:14:51,818
to show your wealth to have it.

471
00:14:52,168 --> 00:14:53,189
Teach it this week.

472
00:14:53,369 --> 00:14:56,005
When your child compares themselves with a

473
00:14:56,005 --> 00:14:58,452
friend, ask, what do you actually want,

474
00:14:58,452 --> 00:14:58,954
and why?

475
00:14:59,134 --> 00:15:01,682
For yourself, unfollow a few accounts that

476
00:15:01,682 --> 00:15:03,685
make you feel like you need more.

477
00:15:04,035 --> 00:15:05,393
Lesson number twelve.

478
00:15:05,573 --> 00:15:07,802
Money is something families talk about.

479
00:15:08,152 --> 00:15:09,733
In many wealthy families,

480
00:15:09,733 --> 00:15:11,251
money is a normal topic.

481
00:15:11,431 --> 00:15:14,063
Kids hear their parents discuss investments,

482
00:15:14,063 --> 00:15:15,379
budgets and decisions.

483
00:15:15,559 --> 00:15:17,382
They learn the vocabulary early.

484
00:15:17,562 --> 00:15:19,040
Words like interest,

485
00:15:19,040 --> 00:15:22,366
dividend, net worth and budget feel familiar,

486
00:15:22,366 --> 00:15:23,622
not intimidating.

487
00:15:23,972 --> 00:15:26,861
Talking about money doesn't mean sharing every

488
00:15:26,861 --> 00:15:29,626
detail or burdening kids with adult worries.

489
00:15:29,806 --> 00:15:33,275
It means explaining decisions in age appropriate

490
00:15:33,275 --> 00:15:33,637
ways.

491
00:15:33,817 --> 00:15:35,547
We're not buying a new car because

492
00:15:35,547 --> 00:15:37,277
we'd rather save for your college.

493
00:15:37,457 --> 00:15:39,693
That one sentence teaches more than a

494
00:15:39,693 --> 00:15:40,963
semester of lectures.

495
00:15:41,313 --> 00:15:42,369
Teach it this week.

496
00:15:42,549 --> 00:15:45,495
At dinner, explain one money decision your

497
00:15:45,495 --> 00:15:46,969
family made recently,

498
00:15:46,969 --> 00:15:47,530
and why.

499
00:15:47,710 --> 00:15:50,824
For yourself, have one honest money conversation

500
00:15:50,824 --> 00:15:52,447
with a partner or friend.

501
00:15:52,797 --> 00:15:54,225
Lesson number thirteen.

502
00:15:54,405 --> 00:15:56,065
Let them make small mistakes now.

503
00:15:56,415 --> 00:15:58,612
Some of the best money lessons come

504
00:15:58,612 --> 00:15:59,491
from mistakes.

505
00:15:59,671 --> 00:16:01,863
Spending a whole allowance on the first

506
00:16:01,863 --> 00:16:03,831
day and having nothing left for the

507
00:16:03,831 --> 00:16:04,280
weekend.

508
00:16:04,460 --> 00:16:06,124
Buying a toy that breaks in a

509
00:16:06,124 --> 00:16:06,411
week.

510
00:16:06,591 --> 00:16:09,270
Wealthy parents often resist the urge to

511
00:16:09,270 --> 00:16:11,815
rescue kids from these small mistakes.

512
00:16:12,166 --> 00:16:14,044
A twenty dollar mistake at ten is

513
00:16:14,044 --> 00:16:16,377
far cheaper than a twenty thousand dollar

514
00:16:16,377 --> 00:16:17,402
mistake at thirty.

515
00:16:17,582 --> 00:16:20,604
When kids experience the consequences of money

516
00:16:20,604 --> 00:16:22,903
choices while the stakes are small,

517
00:16:22,903 --> 00:16:25,860
they build judgment that protects them later.

518
00:16:26,210 --> 00:16:27,301
Teach it this week.

519
00:16:27,481 --> 00:16:29,363
Next time your child makes a money

520
00:16:29,363 --> 00:16:30,581
mistake, don't fix it.

521
00:16:30,761 --> 00:16:32,628
Ask what they learned and what they'd

522
00:16:32,628 --> 00:16:33,385
do differently.

523
00:16:33,565 --> 00:16:35,836
For yourself, write down your biggest money

524
00:16:35,836 --> 00:16:37,791
mistake and the lesson it taught you.

525
00:16:38,141 --> 00:16:39,685
Lesson number fourteen.

526
00:16:39,865 --> 00:16:41,119
Knowing what's enough.

527
00:16:41,469 --> 00:16:44,490
Some wealthy families talk openly about the

528
00:16:44,490 --> 00:16:45,544
idea of enough.

529
00:16:45,724 --> 00:16:47,372
Without a sense of enough,

530
00:16:47,372 --> 00:16:49,527
no amount of money ever feels like

531
00:16:49,527 --> 00:16:50,542
it's sufficient.

532
00:16:50,722 --> 00:16:52,655
There's always a bigger house,

533
00:16:52,655 --> 00:16:53,428
a nicer car,

534
00:16:53,428 --> 00:16:54,588
a better vacation.

535
00:16:54,768 --> 00:16:56,768
With it, people can enjoy what they

536
00:16:56,768 --> 00:16:58,483
have and make clearer choices.

537
00:16:58,833 --> 00:17:01,478
Teaching kids about enough helps them resist

538
00:17:01,478 --> 00:17:04,544
endless consumption, and protects them from feeling

539
00:17:04,544 --> 00:17:06,588
poor no matter how much they earn.

540
00:17:06,768 --> 00:17:08,999
It's one of the most valuable gifts

541
00:17:08,999 --> 00:17:10,147
a parent can give.

542
00:17:10,497 --> 00:17:11,425
Teach it this week.

543
00:17:11,605 --> 00:17:12,381
Ask your child,

544
00:17:12,381 --> 00:17:13,881
what would be enough for you?

545
00:17:14,061 --> 00:17:15,525
A toy, a phone,

546
00:17:15,525 --> 00:17:16,209
a room?

547
00:17:16,389 --> 00:17:18,141
Talk about how it feels to have

548
00:17:18,141 --> 00:17:18,537
enough.

549
00:17:18,717 --> 00:17:21,015
For yourself, write down what enough looks

550
00:17:21,015 --> 00:17:22,165
like for your family.

551
00:17:22,515 --> 00:17:24,152
Lesson number fifteen.

552
00:17:24,332 --> 00:17:25,393
Money is a tool,

553
00:17:25,393 --> 00:17:27,118
not a measure of a person.

554
00:17:27,468 --> 00:17:30,085
Rich parents who raise grounded kids often

555
00:17:30,085 --> 00:17:32,828
teach that money doesn't make someone better

556
00:17:32,828 --> 00:17:33,389
or worse.

557
00:17:33,569 --> 00:17:35,187
It's a tool for security,

558
00:17:35,187 --> 00:17:36,936
freedom and helping others.

559
00:17:37,116 --> 00:17:39,352
Kids who see money as a scoreboard

560
00:17:39,352 --> 00:17:41,458
can grow up anxious or arrogant.

561
00:17:41,638 --> 00:17:43,351
Kids who see it as a tool

562
00:17:43,351 --> 00:17:45,202
tend to use it more wisely.

563
00:17:45,552 --> 00:17:47,537
This lesson shows up in how parents

564
00:17:47,537 --> 00:17:48,559
talk about others.

565
00:17:48,739 --> 00:17:50,729
Do they admire people for their cars,

566
00:17:50,729 --> 00:17:51,966
or for their character?

567
00:17:52,146 --> 00:17:53,249
Kids notice.

568
00:17:53,429 --> 00:17:55,389
And they absorb those values far more

569
00:17:55,389 --> 00:17:57,191
from watching than from listening.

570
00:17:57,541 --> 00:17:58,504
Teach it this week.

571
00:17:58,684 --> 00:18:01,125
Point out someone you admire for their

572
00:18:01,125 --> 00:18:03,374
kindness, work ethic or generosity,

573
00:18:03,374 --> 00:18:04,466
not their wealth.

574
00:18:04,646 --> 00:18:07,092
For yourself, notice when you judge people

575
00:18:07,092 --> 00:18:08,082
by what they own.

576
00:18:08,433 --> 00:18:09,837
Let me pause for a second.

577
00:18:10,017 --> 00:18:11,832
If you're finding this useful,

578
00:18:11,832 --> 00:18:14,251
whether you're a parent or just learning

579
00:18:14,251 --> 00:18:15,884
these lessons for yourself,

580
00:18:15,884 --> 00:18:18,121
please subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

581
00:18:18,301 --> 00:18:20,246
It's free and it helps the channel

582
00:18:20,246 --> 00:18:21,389
reach more families.

583
00:18:21,569 --> 00:18:23,381
Now, lesson sixteen.

584
00:18:23,731 --> 00:18:25,344
Lesson number sixteen.

585
00:18:25,524 --> 00:18:26,860
Give some of it away.

586
00:18:27,210 --> 00:18:29,408
Many wealthy families make giving part of

587
00:18:29,408 --> 00:18:31,714
their money system from the very beginning.

588
00:18:31,894 --> 00:18:33,450
Remember the three jars.

589
00:18:33,630 --> 00:18:36,358
Save, spend and give.

590
00:18:36,538 --> 00:18:38,937
Kids learn that money can help others,

591
00:18:38,937 --> 00:18:40,957
and that generosity is part of a

592
00:18:40,957 --> 00:18:41,589
good life.

593
00:18:41,769 --> 00:18:44,547
Stanley found that many millionaires gave regularly

594
00:18:44,547 --> 00:18:46,018
to causes they cared about.

595
00:18:46,368 --> 00:18:48,748
Giving also changes how kids see their

596
00:18:48,748 --> 00:18:49,375
own money.

597
00:18:49,555 --> 00:18:52,004
It builds gratitude and perspective.

598
00:18:52,184 --> 00:18:54,561
And research on happiness has found that

599
00:18:54,561 --> 00:18:56,938
spending money on others can make people

600
00:18:56,938 --> 00:18:59,255
happier than spending it on themselves.

601
00:18:59,605 --> 00:19:00,580
Teach it this week.

602
00:19:00,760 --> 00:19:02,963
Let your child choose a cause for

603
00:19:02,963 --> 00:19:03,964
their give jar.

604
00:19:04,144 --> 00:19:05,880
A local animal shelter,

605
00:19:05,880 --> 00:19:06,785
a food bank,

606
00:19:06,785 --> 00:19:08,068
a friend in need.

607
00:19:08,248 --> 00:19:10,846
For yourself, set a small regular giving

608
00:19:10,846 --> 00:19:13,055
amount, even five dollars a month.

609
00:19:13,405 --> 00:19:14,972
Lesson number seventeen.

610
00:19:15,152 --> 00:19:17,323
Real wealth is usually quiet.

611
00:19:17,673 --> 00:19:19,988
Many wealthy parents teach their kids not

612
00:19:19,988 --> 00:19:22,190
to talk about money outside the family,

613
00:19:22,190 --> 00:19:23,489
and not to show it off.

614
00:19:23,669 --> 00:19:25,736
It's partly about safety,

615
00:19:25,736 --> 00:19:27,721
and partly about values.

616
00:19:27,901 --> 00:19:29,863
They don't want their kids to feel

617
00:19:29,863 --> 00:19:31,998
superior, or to be targets for people

618
00:19:31,998 --> 00:19:33,671
who want something from them.

619
00:19:34,022 --> 00:19:36,638
This lesson also helps kids understand that

620
00:19:36,638 --> 00:19:39,071
the flashy people they see online aren't

621
00:19:39,071 --> 00:19:40,105
necessarily rich.

622
00:19:40,285 --> 00:19:42,869
As Morgan Housel wrote in The Psychology

623
00:19:42,869 --> 00:19:45,066
of Money, wealth is what you don't

624
00:19:45,066 --> 00:19:45,324
see.

625
00:19:45,504 --> 00:19:46,955
It's the money not spent.

626
00:19:47,306 --> 00:19:48,385
Teach it this week.

627
00:19:48,565 --> 00:19:50,767
Talk with your kids about why it's

628
00:19:50,767 --> 00:19:53,163
wise to keep family finances private.

629
00:19:53,343 --> 00:19:56,469
For yourself, notice the difference between people

630
00:19:56,469 --> 00:19:58,471
who look rich and people who are

631
00:19:58,471 --> 00:19:59,659
financially secure.

632
00:20:00,009 --> 00:20:01,959
Here's the hardest truth for parents.

633
00:20:02,139 --> 00:20:03,821
Kids learn far more from what you

634
00:20:03,821 --> 00:20:05,146
do than from what you say.

635
00:20:05,326 --> 00:20:07,702
You can explain compound interest perfectly,

636
00:20:07,702 --> 00:20:09,701
but if you buy everything on impulse,

637
00:20:09,701 --> 00:20:11,375
that's the lesson they'll keep.

638
00:20:11,555 --> 00:20:14,021
The Cambridge research found that kids pick

639
00:20:14,021 --> 00:20:16,258
up money habits largely by watching the

640
00:20:16,258 --> 00:20:17,348
adults around them.

641
00:20:17,699 --> 00:20:19,370
That's actually good news.

642
00:20:19,550 --> 00:20:21,652
It means you don't need perfect lessons.

643
00:20:21,832 --> 00:20:23,515
You need to model the behavior.

644
00:20:23,695 --> 00:20:25,906
Let them see you compare prices,

645
00:20:25,906 --> 00:20:27,632
say no to an impulse buy,

646
00:20:27,632 --> 00:20:29,221
put money into savings,

647
00:20:29,221 --> 00:20:30,534
and give to others.

648
00:20:30,714 --> 00:20:33,500
Narrate your decisions out loud sometimes.

649
00:20:33,680 --> 00:20:34,327
I'd like that,

650
00:20:34,327 --> 00:20:35,760
but I'm going to wait and think

651
00:20:35,760 --> 00:20:36,176
about it.

652
00:20:36,526 --> 00:20:38,291
And if your own money habits aren't

653
00:20:38,291 --> 00:20:39,300
where you want them,

654
00:20:39,300 --> 00:20:41,167
the best lesson might be letting kids

655
00:20:41,167 --> 00:20:42,075
watch you improve.

656
00:20:42,255 --> 00:20:44,219
Seeing a parent pay off debt,

657
00:20:44,219 --> 00:20:46,454
build savings and stick to a plan

658
00:20:46,454 --> 00:20:49,366
teaches resilience, and that it's never too

659
00:20:49,366 --> 00:20:50,382
late to change.

660
00:20:50,732 --> 00:20:51,707
That's angle two.

661
00:20:51,887 --> 00:20:53,746
Waiting, not comparing,

662
00:20:53,746 --> 00:20:55,039
open money talk,

663
00:20:55,039 --> 00:20:56,898
small mistakes, enough,

664
00:20:56,898 --> 00:20:58,192
money as a tool,

665
00:20:58,192 --> 00:21:00,455
generosity and quiet wealth.

666
00:21:00,635 --> 00:21:01,877
Now angle three.

667
00:21:02,057 --> 00:21:02,789
The playbook.

668
00:21:02,969 --> 00:21:05,699
Eight practical tools wealthy parents use to

669
00:21:05,699 --> 00:21:07,995
give their kids a real financial head

670
00:21:07,995 --> 00:21:08,367
start.

671
00:21:08,718 --> 00:21:11,352
The playbook lessons are where wealthy families

672
00:21:11,352 --> 00:21:12,305
often pull ahead.

673
00:21:12,485 --> 00:21:14,726
Not because they have secret tools,

674
00:21:14,726 --> 00:21:17,224
but because they know these tools exist

675
00:21:17,224 --> 00:21:18,441
and use them early.

676
00:21:18,621 --> 00:21:20,709
Most of them are available to any

677
00:21:20,709 --> 00:21:21,152
family.

678
00:21:21,502 --> 00:21:23,022
Lesson number eighteen.

679
00:21:23,202 --> 00:21:24,410
Open accounts early.

680
00:21:24,760 --> 00:21:27,755
Many wealthy parents open savings and investment

681
00:21:27,755 --> 00:21:29,253
accounts for their kids.

682
00:21:29,433 --> 00:21:32,358
Custodial accounts let parents invest on a

683
00:21:32,358 --> 00:21:33,403
child's behalf.

684
00:21:33,583 --> 00:21:35,924
And if a child has earned income,

685
00:21:35,924 --> 00:21:37,768
from a job or babysitting,

686
00:21:37,768 --> 00:21:40,037
for example, they may be able to

687
00:21:40,037 --> 00:21:42,519
contribute to a custodial Roth IRA,

688
00:21:42,519 --> 00:21:44,718
up to the amount they earned or

689
00:21:44,718 --> 00:21:45,924
the annual limit,

690
00:21:45,924 --> 00:21:47,271
whichever is lower.

691
00:21:47,451 --> 00:21:49,518
Check the current IRS rules.

692
00:21:49,867 --> 00:21:52,128
A Roth IRA started in the teenage

693
00:21:52,128 --> 00:21:54,800
years has decades of potential tax free

694
00:21:54,800 --> 00:21:56,102
growth ahead of it.

695
00:21:56,282 --> 00:21:58,713
Even a few thousand dollars invested at

696
00:21:58,713 --> 00:22:01,518
fifteen can grow significantly by retirement.

697
00:22:01,698 --> 00:22:04,943
Some parents even match their kids' contributions,

698
00:22:04,943 --> 00:22:06,435
like an employer match.

699
00:22:06,784 --> 00:22:07,737
Teach it this week.

700
00:22:07,917 --> 00:22:09,853
If your teenager has a job,

701
00:22:09,853 --> 00:22:12,865
research custodial Roth IRA options with a

702
00:22:12,865 --> 00:22:13,582
brokerage.

703
00:22:13,762 --> 00:22:16,444
Consider matching part of what they contribute.

704
00:22:16,624 --> 00:22:19,377
For yourself, make sure you're using your

705
00:22:19,377 --> 00:22:21,256
own tax advantaged accounts.

706
00:22:21,606 --> 00:22:23,174
Lesson number nineteen.

707
00:22:23,354 --> 00:22:25,409
Understand credit before you need it.

708
00:22:25,759 --> 00:22:28,461
A credit score affects the interest rate

709
00:22:28,461 --> 00:22:30,285
on car loans and mortgages,

710
00:22:30,285 --> 00:22:34,002
and sometimes rental applications and insurance prices.

711
00:22:34,182 --> 00:22:37,267
Wealthy parents often explain how credit scores

712
00:22:37,267 --> 00:22:39,499
work before their kids leave home.

713
00:22:39,679 --> 00:22:40,511
Pay on time,

714
00:22:40,511 --> 00:22:41,759
keep balances low,

715
00:22:41,759 --> 00:22:44,185
and don't open too many accounts at

716
00:22:44,185 --> 00:22:44,532
once.

717
00:22:44,882 --> 00:22:47,309
Some parents add their teenager as an

718
00:22:47,309 --> 00:22:49,737
authorized user on a card they manage

719
00:22:49,737 --> 00:22:50,524
responsibly.

720
00:22:50,704 --> 00:22:52,522
Depending on the card issuer,

721
00:22:52,522 --> 00:22:54,466
this can help the child start a

722
00:22:54,466 --> 00:22:55,406
credit history.

723
00:22:55,586 --> 00:22:57,830
Others help their young adult open a

724
00:22:57,830 --> 00:22:59,762
secured card and use it for one

725
00:22:59,762 --> 00:23:02,006
small bill paid in full every month.

726
00:23:02,357 --> 00:23:03,332
Teach it this week.

727
00:23:03,512 --> 00:23:05,835
Show your teen what a credit score

728
00:23:05,835 --> 00:23:08,021
is and pull your own free credit

729
00:23:08,021 --> 00:23:10,617
report together at annualcreditreport.

730
00:23:10,797 --> 00:23:11,064
com.

731
00:23:11,244 --> 00:23:13,676
For yourself, set every card to autopay

732
00:23:13,676 --> 00:23:14,985
at least the minimum,

733
00:23:14,985 --> 00:23:16,294
so you're never late.

734
00:23:16,644 --> 00:23:17,945
Lesson number twenty.

735
00:23:18,125 --> 00:23:19,739
You can ask for a better deal.

736
00:23:20,088 --> 00:23:22,908
Wealthy parents often let their kids watch

737
00:23:22,908 --> 00:23:25,660
them negotiate, whether it's a car price,

738
00:23:25,660 --> 00:23:26,533
a phone bill,

739
00:23:26,533 --> 00:23:27,472
or a contract.

740
00:23:27,652 --> 00:23:29,993
Kids learn that the first number isn't

741
00:23:29,993 --> 00:23:32,458
always final, and that a polite question

742
00:23:32,458 --> 00:23:33,690
can save real money.

743
00:23:34,040 --> 00:23:37,081
Negotiation is also a career skill.

744
00:23:37,261 --> 00:23:40,225
Research has shown that starting salaries affect

745
00:23:40,225 --> 00:23:41,645
a lifetime of earnings,

746
00:23:41,645 --> 00:23:43,990
because raises are often based on what

747
00:23:43,990 --> 00:23:45,040
you already make.

748
00:23:45,220 --> 00:23:47,980
Young people who negotiate their first salary

749
00:23:47,980 --> 00:23:49,330
can benefit for years.

750
00:23:49,680 --> 00:23:50,632
Teach it this week.

751
00:23:50,812 --> 00:23:52,784
Let your kids listen while you call

752
00:23:52,784 --> 00:23:54,248
to ask for a lower rate on

753
00:23:54,248 --> 00:23:54,643
a bill.

754
00:23:54,823 --> 00:23:56,437
Talk afterward about what worked.

755
00:23:56,617 --> 00:23:59,288
For yourself, research the salary range for

756
00:23:59,288 --> 00:24:01,400
your role before your next review.

757
00:24:01,750 --> 00:24:03,270
Lesson number twenty one.

758
00:24:03,450 --> 00:24:04,948
Protect what you build.

759
00:24:05,299 --> 00:24:08,166
Rich families tend to understand insurance.

760
00:24:08,346 --> 00:24:09,825
Not insuring everything,

761
00:24:09,825 --> 00:24:12,660
but protecting against disasters that would be

762
00:24:12,660 --> 00:24:14,140
financially devastating.

763
00:24:14,320 --> 00:24:16,583
Health insurance, car insurance,

764
00:24:16,583 --> 00:24:18,847
renters or homeowners insurance,

765
00:24:18,847 --> 00:24:21,676
and life insurance when others depend on

766
00:24:21,676 --> 00:24:21,959
you.

767
00:24:22,309 --> 00:24:25,297
Young adults often skip renters insurance or

768
00:24:25,297 --> 00:24:27,947
choose the bare minimum car coverage to

769
00:24:27,947 --> 00:24:28,694
save money.

770
00:24:28,874 --> 00:24:31,426
Then one accident or theft sets them

771
00:24:31,426 --> 00:24:32,206
back years.

772
00:24:32,386 --> 00:24:34,891
Teaching kids how insurance works before they

773
00:24:34,891 --> 00:24:36,450
leave home can prevent that.

774
00:24:36,800 --> 00:24:37,833
Teach it this week.

775
00:24:38,013 --> 00:24:40,431
Walk your teen through your car or

776
00:24:40,431 --> 00:24:41,995
home insurance policy.

777
00:24:42,175 --> 00:24:43,208
What does it cover?

778
00:24:43,388 --> 00:24:44,538
What's a deductible?

779
00:24:44,718 --> 00:24:46,986
For yourself, check that your coverage still

780
00:24:46,986 --> 00:24:47,760
fits your life.

781
00:24:48,110 --> 00:24:49,642
Lesson number twenty two.

782
00:24:49,822 --> 00:24:51,645
Try starting something of your own.

783
00:24:51,995 --> 00:24:55,828
Many self made millionaires started small businesses

784
00:24:55,828 --> 00:24:56,418
as kids.

785
00:24:56,598 --> 00:24:58,975
Lemonade stands, lawn mowing,

786
00:24:58,975 --> 00:25:02,171
selling crafts, reselling items online.

787
00:25:02,351 --> 00:25:05,243
Stanley found that many millionaires were business

788
00:25:05,243 --> 00:25:05,648
owners.

789
00:25:05,828 --> 00:25:08,588
Early entrepreneurship teaches pricing,

790
00:25:08,588 --> 00:25:11,064
customer service, costs and profit.

791
00:25:11,414 --> 00:25:13,873
Even a tiny business teaches lessons a

792
00:25:13,873 --> 00:25:14,908
classroom can't.

793
00:25:15,088 --> 00:25:17,050
What happens when you price too high?

794
00:25:17,230 --> 00:25:17,776
Too low?

795
00:25:17,956 --> 00:25:19,361
What are your costs?

796
00:25:19,541 --> 00:25:22,037
What did you actually make after expenses?

797
00:25:22,217 --> 00:25:25,043
Those questions build business thinking that lasts

798
00:25:25,043 --> 00:25:25,665
a lifetime.

799
00:25:26,015 --> 00:25:27,036
Teach it this week.

800
00:25:27,216 --> 00:25:29,587
Help your child start a tiny business,

801
00:25:29,587 --> 00:25:30,897
even for one weekend.

802
00:25:31,077 --> 00:25:34,200
Track costs, sales and profit together.

803
00:25:34,380 --> 00:25:37,217
For yourself, consider one skill you could

804
00:25:37,217 --> 00:25:39,244
turn into a small side income.

805
00:25:39,595 --> 00:25:41,034
Lesson number twenty three.

806
00:25:41,214 --> 00:25:42,951
If it sounds too good to be

807
00:25:42,951 --> 00:25:44,302
true, it probably is.

808
00:25:44,653 --> 00:25:46,339
Scams target every age,

809
00:25:46,339 --> 00:25:49,052
including teenagers and young adults,

810
00:25:49,052 --> 00:25:51,032
often through social media,

811
00:25:51,032 --> 00:25:53,012
gaming platforms and texts.

812
00:25:53,192 --> 00:25:56,125
The Federal Trade Commission has reported that

813
00:25:56,125 --> 00:25:58,866
younger adults report losing money to fraud

814
00:25:58,866 --> 00:26:01,162
more often than older adults in some

815
00:26:01,162 --> 00:26:03,712
categories, even if older adults tend to

816
00:26:03,712 --> 00:26:04,987
lose larger amounts.

817
00:26:05,338 --> 00:26:08,127
Wealthy parents teach kids to be skeptical

818
00:26:08,127 --> 00:26:09,589
of guaranteed returns,

819
00:26:09,589 --> 00:26:12,378
urgent deadlines, requests for gift cards,

820
00:26:12,378 --> 00:26:15,102
and anyone asking for passwords or codes.

821
00:26:15,282 --> 00:26:17,828
These red flags apply to almost every

822
00:26:17,828 --> 00:26:18,172
scam.

823
00:26:18,523 --> 00:26:19,544
Teach it this week.

824
00:26:19,724 --> 00:26:22,011
Share three questions with your kids.

825
00:26:22,191 --> 00:26:23,527
Is someone rushing me?

826
00:26:23,707 --> 00:26:25,973
Are they asking for gift cards,

827
00:26:25,973 --> 00:26:27,143
crypto or codes?

828
00:26:27,323 --> 00:26:28,844
Does it promise easy money?

829
00:26:29,024 --> 00:26:30,035
If yes to any,

830
00:26:30,035 --> 00:26:31,625
stop and ask a parent.

831
00:26:31,805 --> 00:26:34,347
For yourself, use the same questions.

832
00:26:34,698 --> 00:26:36,358
Lesson number twenty four.

833
00:26:36,538 --> 00:26:38,128
Have a plan for the family.

834
00:26:38,479 --> 00:26:40,911
Wealthy families usually have wills,

835
00:26:40,911 --> 00:26:43,073
named beneficiaries on accounts,

836
00:26:43,073 --> 00:26:44,493
and sometimes trusts.

837
00:26:44,673 --> 00:26:47,016
Many explain the basics to their kids

838
00:26:47,016 --> 00:26:48,030
as they grow up,

839
00:26:48,030 --> 00:26:50,880
so the next generation understands how wealth

840
00:26:50,880 --> 00:26:52,591
is protected and passed on.

841
00:26:52,941 --> 00:26:54,845
Every family can do the basics.

842
00:26:55,025 --> 00:26:57,955
A will, beneficiaries on retirement accounts and

843
00:26:57,955 --> 00:27:00,031
life insurance, and a plan for who

844
00:27:00,031 --> 00:27:01,863
would care for young children.

845
00:27:02,043 --> 00:27:03,401
These aren't just for the rich.

846
00:27:03,581 --> 00:27:06,344
They protect any family from confusion and

847
00:27:06,344 --> 00:27:06,937
conflict.

848
00:27:07,287 --> 00:27:08,285
Teach it this week.

849
00:27:08,465 --> 00:27:10,000
If your kids are older,

850
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:12,337
explain in simple terms what a will

851
00:27:12,337 --> 00:27:13,806
is and why it matters.

852
00:27:13,986 --> 00:27:16,613
For yourself, check the beneficiaries on every

853
00:27:16,613 --> 00:27:17,585
account you have.

854
00:27:17,935 --> 00:27:19,641
And lesson number twenty five.

855
00:27:19,821 --> 00:27:22,166
Rich parents don't give their kids everything.

856
00:27:22,517 --> 00:27:24,107
This one surprises people.

857
00:27:24,287 --> 00:27:25,962
In The Millionaire Next Door,

858
00:27:25,962 --> 00:27:28,966
Stanley described what he called economic outpatient

859
00:27:28,966 --> 00:27:31,854
care, the substantial financial gifts some parents

860
00:27:31,854 --> 00:27:33,529
give to their adult children.

861
00:27:33,709 --> 00:27:36,719
His research suggested that adult children who

862
00:27:36,719 --> 00:27:40,057
received large ongoing gifts often accumulated less

863
00:27:40,057 --> 00:27:42,544
wealth than similar people who didn't.

864
00:27:42,894 --> 00:27:45,251
The lesson many wealthy parents take from

865
00:27:45,251 --> 00:27:47,378
this is that independence is the real

866
00:27:47,378 --> 00:27:47,665
gift.

867
00:27:47,845 --> 00:27:49,900
They help with education or a first

868
00:27:49,900 --> 00:27:52,072
home, perhaps, but they don't fund an

869
00:27:52,072 --> 00:27:53,070
entire lifestyle.

870
00:27:53,250 --> 00:27:55,189
They want their kids to know how

871
00:27:55,189 --> 00:27:57,129
to earn, save and build on their

872
00:27:57,129 --> 00:27:57,371
own.

873
00:27:57,722 --> 00:27:58,708
Teach it this week.

874
00:27:58,888 --> 00:28:00,707
Think about one thing you do for

875
00:28:00,707 --> 00:28:02,697
your child that they could learn to

876
00:28:02,697 --> 00:28:04,914
do themselves, with money or otherwise.

877
00:28:05,094 --> 00:28:06,017
Hand it over,

878
00:28:06,017 --> 00:28:06,940
with support.

879
00:28:07,120 --> 00:28:09,344
For yourself, ask whether you depend on

880
00:28:09,344 --> 00:28:10,998
help that's holding you back.

881
00:28:11,347 --> 00:28:13,542
Let's put this into a simple timeline.

882
00:28:13,722 --> 00:28:15,254
Ages three to five.

883
00:28:15,434 --> 00:28:16,623
Coins and counting,

884
00:28:16,623 --> 00:28:18,626
the idea that things cost money,

885
00:28:18,626 --> 00:28:20,879
and that you sometimes have to wait.

886
00:28:21,059 --> 00:28:22,278
Ages six to ten.

887
00:28:22,458 --> 00:28:23,526
The three jars,

888
00:28:23,526 --> 00:28:24,949
small savings goals,

889
00:28:24,949 --> 00:28:28,008
and the difference between needs and wants.

890
00:28:28,188 --> 00:28:29,941
Ages eleven to thirteen.

891
00:28:30,121 --> 00:28:32,907
An allowance with real responsibilities,

892
00:28:32,907 --> 00:28:35,903
comparison shopping, and compound interest.

893
00:28:36,252 --> 00:28:38,319
Ages fourteen to eighteen.

894
00:28:38,499 --> 00:28:39,383
A first job,

895
00:28:39,383 --> 00:28:40,783
reading a pay stub,

896
00:28:40,783 --> 00:28:43,435
a first bank account and debit card,

897
00:28:43,435 --> 00:28:45,866
a custodial Roth IRA if they have

898
00:28:45,866 --> 00:28:48,223
earned income, how credit works,

899
00:28:48,223 --> 00:28:48,960
and scams.

900
00:28:49,140 --> 00:28:50,811
Ages eighteen and up.

901
00:28:50,991 --> 00:28:52,534
Budgeting on their own,

902
00:28:52,534 --> 00:28:54,413
building credit responsibly,

903
00:28:54,413 --> 00:28:57,968
choosing insurance, using an employer retirement plan

904
00:28:57,968 --> 00:28:59,712
from their very first job,

905
00:28:59,712 --> 00:29:02,663
and taking full responsibility for their own

906
00:29:02,663 --> 00:29:03,066
money.

907
00:29:03,415 --> 00:29:05,677
Maybe you're watching this and thinking,

908
00:29:05,677 --> 00:29:07,316
nobody taught me any of this.

909
00:29:07,496 --> 00:29:08,437
You're not alone.

910
00:29:08,617 --> 00:29:11,228
A growing number of states now require

911
00:29:11,228 --> 00:29:14,046
a personal finance course in high school,

912
00:29:14,046 --> 00:29:15,833
but for most adults today,

913
00:29:15,833 --> 00:29:17,139
that came too late.

914
00:29:17,319 --> 00:29:19,850
Most people learned by trial and error.

915
00:29:20,199 --> 00:29:22,004
The good news is that every one

916
00:29:22,004 --> 00:29:24,215
of these twenty five lessons works for

917
00:29:24,215 --> 00:29:24,855
adults too.

918
00:29:25,035 --> 00:29:27,494
Start with three jars of your own,

919
00:29:27,494 --> 00:29:29,157
as automatic transfers.

920
00:29:29,337 --> 00:29:31,775
Learn compound interest with a calculator.

921
00:29:31,955 --> 00:29:33,104
Check your credit report.

922
00:29:33,284 --> 00:29:35,409
Open a retirement account if you haven't.

923
00:29:35,589 --> 00:29:37,796
It's never too late to give yourself

924
00:29:37,796 --> 00:29:39,942
the money education you didn't get.

925
00:29:40,292 --> 00:29:41,972
And if you do have kids,

926
00:29:41,972 --> 00:29:44,000
nieces, nephews or grandkids,

927
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:45,680
you can break the cycle.

928
00:29:45,860 --> 00:29:47,812
The lessons you learn now can become

929
00:29:47,812 --> 00:29:50,416
the kitchen table conversations that change your

930
00:29:50,416 --> 00:29:52,152
family's future for generations.

931
00:29:52,503 --> 00:29:53,396
Question one.

932
00:29:53,576 --> 00:29:55,434
Should allowance be tied to chores?

933
00:29:55,614 --> 00:29:57,367
Families disagree on this.

934
00:29:57,547 --> 00:29:59,206
Some pay for all chores,

935
00:29:59,206 --> 00:30:02,041
some give an allowance for learning money

936
00:30:02,041 --> 00:30:04,668
skills and expect basic chores as part

937
00:30:04,668 --> 00:30:06,120
of being in a family,

938
00:30:06,120 --> 00:30:08,263
with extra paid jobs available.

939
00:30:08,443 --> 00:30:10,761
Either way, the key is consistency and

940
00:30:10,761 --> 00:30:12,286
using the money to teach.

941
00:30:12,636 --> 00:30:13,495
Question two.

942
00:30:13,675 --> 00:30:15,428
How much should I tell my kids

943
00:30:15,428 --> 00:30:17,065
about our family's finances?

944
00:30:17,245 --> 00:30:19,151
Share values and decisions,

945
00:30:19,151 --> 00:30:20,774
adjusted for their age.

946
00:30:20,954 --> 00:30:23,359
You don't need to share exact salaries

947
00:30:23,359 --> 00:30:24,625
with young children,

948
00:30:24,625 --> 00:30:27,536
but older teens can benefit from understanding

949
00:30:27,536 --> 00:30:29,941
real numbers, like how much the family

950
00:30:29,941 --> 00:30:31,902
spends on groceries or housing.

951
00:30:32,253 --> 00:30:33,402
Question three.

952
00:30:33,582 --> 00:30:35,325
What if I'm not good with money

953
00:30:35,325 --> 00:30:35,718
myself?

954
00:30:35,898 --> 00:30:36,955
Then learn together.

955
00:30:37,135 --> 00:30:39,561
Watching a parent learn and improve is

956
00:30:39,561 --> 00:30:40,711
a powerful lesson.

957
00:30:40,891 --> 00:30:42,226
You don't need to be perfect.

958
00:30:42,406 --> 00:30:44,377
You need to be honest and willing

959
00:30:44,377 --> 00:30:44,855
to grow.

960
00:30:45,206 --> 00:30:46,227
Question four.

961
00:30:46,407 --> 00:30:48,492
When should my child get a debit

962
00:30:48,492 --> 00:30:49,925
card or a credit card?

963
00:30:50,105 --> 00:30:52,466
Many families start with a debit card

964
00:30:52,466 --> 00:30:54,699
linked to a teen account around the

965
00:30:54,699 --> 00:30:55,975
early teenage years,

966
00:30:55,975 --> 00:30:57,443
with parental controls.

967
00:30:57,623 --> 00:31:00,028
A credit card usually comes later,

968
00:31:00,028 --> 00:31:02,433
often as an authorized user first,

969
00:31:02,433 --> 00:31:04,697
and then their own card in early

970
00:31:04,697 --> 00:31:07,740
adulthood, used for small purchases paid in

971
00:31:07,740 --> 00:31:08,942
full every month.

972
00:31:09,122 --> 00:31:11,515
The goal is practice with guardrails before

973
00:31:11,515 --> 00:31:12,628
the stakes are high.

974
00:31:12,979 --> 00:31:13,989
Question five.

975
00:31:14,169 --> 00:31:16,061
Should I pay for my kids' college?

976
00:31:16,241 --> 00:31:17,683
That's a personal choice,

977
00:31:17,683 --> 00:31:19,701
and many families help in some way.

978
00:31:19,881 --> 00:31:23,452
But many financial planners suggest prioritizing your

979
00:31:23,452 --> 00:31:25,407
own retirement savings first,

980
00:31:25,407 --> 00:31:28,641
because students can borrow or earn scholarships

981
00:31:28,641 --> 00:31:31,269
for college, but nobody lends you money

982
00:31:31,269 --> 00:31:32,280
for retirement.

983
00:31:32,460 --> 00:31:34,448
A five twenty nine plan is one

984
00:31:34,448 --> 00:31:37,097
tax advantaged way to save for education

985
00:31:37,097 --> 00:31:39,217
if you have room in your budget.

986
00:31:39,568 --> 00:31:40,473
Quick recap.

987
00:31:40,653 --> 00:31:42,604
Angle one, the numbers.

988
00:31:42,784 --> 00:31:43,971
Money is earned,

989
00:31:43,971 --> 00:31:46,196
save first, compound interest,

990
00:31:46,196 --> 00:31:48,051
interest works both ways,

991
00:31:48,051 --> 00:31:49,461
price versus value,

992
00:31:49,461 --> 00:31:52,874
opportunity cost, budgeting with an allowance,

993
00:31:52,874 --> 00:31:54,729
understanding a paycheck,

994
00:31:54,729 --> 00:31:56,658
and owning versus renting.

995
00:31:57,008 --> 00:31:59,028
Angle two, the psychology.

996
00:31:59,208 --> 00:32:00,803
Wait for what you want,

997
00:32:00,803 --> 00:32:03,300
don't compare, money isn't a secret,

998
00:32:03,300 --> 00:32:05,590
small mistakes are cheap tuition,

999
00:32:05,590 --> 00:32:06,907
enough is a number,

1000
00:32:06,907 --> 00:32:08,017
money is a tool,

1001
00:32:08,017 --> 00:32:10,237
generosity, and wealth is quiet.

1002
00:32:10,588 --> 00:32:12,155
Angle three, the playbook.

1003
00:32:12,335 --> 00:32:13,744
Open accounts early,

1004
00:32:13,744 --> 00:32:15,506
credit basics, negotiate,

1005
00:32:15,506 --> 00:32:17,056
protect what you have,

1006
00:32:17,056 --> 00:32:19,029
start something, spot scams,

1007
00:32:19,029 --> 00:32:20,156
the family plan,

1008
00:32:20,156 --> 00:32:22,552
and not everything is handed over.

1009
00:32:22,902 --> 00:32:24,748
The best inheritance isn't money.

1010
00:32:24,928 --> 00:32:26,553
It's knowing how money works.

1011
00:32:26,733 --> 00:32:28,710
Pick one lesson from each angle and

1012
00:32:28,710 --> 00:32:30,688
share it with someone you love this

1013
00:32:30,688 --> 00:32:30,971
week.

1014
00:32:31,151 --> 00:32:32,597
If you want the full plan,

1015
00:32:32,597 --> 00:32:33,710
it's in my playbook,

1016
00:32:33,710 --> 00:32:35,156
linked in the description.

1017
00:32:35,336 --> 00:32:36,983
And tell me in the comments,

1018
00:32:36,983 --> 00:32:39,335
what's one money lesson you wish someone

1019
00:32:39,335 --> 00:32:40,746
had taught you as a kid?

1020
00:32:40,926 --> 00:32:42,215
I'm Ryan Rover.

1021
00:32:42,395 --> 00:32:43,486
See you in the next one.
